    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d115">

                <pb facs="0403" n="1033" sameAs="frus:frus1873p1v2/pg1033"/>

                <head>No. 421.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">General Sickles to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">United
                                States Legation in Spain</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1873-08-27"><hi rendition="#italic">August</hi> 27, 1873</date>.
                        (Received September 18.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 706.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to forward herewith a
                    translation of a note from the minister of state, dated 23d instant, replying to
                    mine of June 15th last, respecting the imposition of fines upon foreign
                    ship-masters for infractions of the peninsular customs regulations. It seems
                    that this government is not disposed to reform the existing legislation on this
                    subject, nor even to reciprocate our usage in the cases of lines imposed on
                    foreign vessels. Under these circumstances it remains to be considered whether
                    we should not modify our liberal course toward Spanish vessels, in the hope that
                    Spain may be better able to appreciate the justice of our complaints when her
                    own ships are exposed to the same inconveniences.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>D. E. SICKLES.</signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d115-a1.xml" xpointer="d115-a1"/>
            </div>
